{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AuHTdaM8rbtD2aq9SkFpHU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n13\t\\usepackage{algpseudocode}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{\n18\t  Anonymous Authors\n19\t}\n20\t\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t%============================================================\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tTemporal abstraction---imagining at variable time scales---is a natural extension of world-model reinforcement learning, yet reliable implementations remain elusive.\n29\tWe present a systematic diagnostic analysis of variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3, identifying why it fails and isolating the causal mechanism.\n30\tThrough a factorial experiment design across three DeepMind Control environments (Walker Walk, Cartpole Swingup, Cheetah Run) with 3 seeds per configuration, we show that a \\emph{trained} dt-embedding reliably induces pathological value overestimation (normalized value/eval ratio $>1.5\\times$ baseline in 4 of 6 trained-embedding seeds across Walker and Cartpole), creating a ``delusional critic'' whose gradients drive the actor toward incorrect policies.\n31\tCollapse is stochastic---occurring in 2 of 9 trained-embedding seeds (22\\%) across environments---but the $>1.5\\times$ diagnostic threshold separates trained from untrained configurations with 83\\% accuracy on Walker (5/6) and 100\\% on the untrained side (6/6).\n32\tThe severity of this mechanism is environment-dependent: environments with higher RSSM prediction error at $\\text{dt}>1$ (Cartpole: $2.14\\times$, Walker: $2.04\\times$ observation gap) exhibit clear collapse, while smoother-dynamics environments (Cheetah: $1.65\\times$ gap) show only gradual degradation.\n33\tWe further demonstrate that restricting imagination rollouts to $\\text{dt}=1$ during multi-dt training---a zero-cost intervention---recovers $90\\% \\pm 5\\%$ (IQM) of baseline performance on Walker Walk with no value overestimation (max nve $= 0.93\\times$).\n34\tOur results provide actionable diagnostic criteria and a practical mitigation guideline for practitioners working with temporal abstraction in world-model RL.\n35\t\\end{abstract}\n36\t\n37\t%============================================================\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\\label{sec:intro}\n40\t\n41\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to reason and act at multiple time scales---has long been a central goal in reinforcement learning \\citep{sutton1999options,precup2000temporal}.\n42\tIn world-model RL, temporal abstraction takes a particularly natural form: instead of modifying the policy or environment interface, one can simply adjust the \\emph{imagination} time step, rolling out the learned dynamics model at variable rates.\n43\tDreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} provides a compelling testbed for this idea, as its Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM) can in principle predict state transitions at arbitrary temporal resolutions by conditioning on a dt-embedding that encodes the elapsed time.\n44\t\n45\tDespite this conceptual elegance, our attempts to realize variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 have consistently failed.\n46\tFixed dt$=$2, fixed dt$=$4, and learned-gate configurations all degrade asymptotic performance---often catastrophically (Section~\\ref{sec:results_degradation}).\n47\tThis paper asks: \\emph{why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL, and what can be done about it?}\n48\t\n49\tOur investigation follows a factorial experiment design that systematically isolates three factors: (i)~whether the dt-embedding is trained or frozen at random initialization, (ii)~whether temporal subsampling is applied to training data, and (iii)~the imagination horizon length.\n50\tThe core finding is a \\textbf{delusional critic} mechanism: when the dt-embedding is trained, the value function learns to exploit precise dt information that the RSSM cannot support at $\\text{dt}>1$, leading to systematic overestimation that feeds back into actor degradation and, stochastically, catastrophic collapse.\n51\t\n52\tWe make three contributions:\n53\t\\begin{enumerate}\n54\t    \\item \\textbf{Diagnostic identification.} We identify trained dt-embedding as the causal trigger for pathological value overestimation ($>1.5\\times$ baseline) and show this threshold reliably separates pathological from healthy configurations.\n55\t    \\item \\textbf{Mechanistic analysis.} We provide RSSM fidelity measurements, actor entropy trajectories, value calibration analysis, and dt-embedding evolution data that together trace the causal chain from embedding divergence through value overestimation to policy collapse.\n56\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical mitigation.} We demonstrate that restricting imagination to $\\text{dt}=1$ during multi-dt training recovers $\\sim$90\\% of baseline performance (IQM across 3 seeds on Walker Walk), providing a zero-cost guideline for practitioners.\n57\t\\end{enumerate}\n58\t\n59\tAll claims are scoped to DreamerV3 and its RSSM architecture.\n60\tWe evaluate across three DMC environments with varying dynamics complexity and report which findings generalize and which remain environment-specific.\n61\t\n62\t%============================================================\n63\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n64\t\\label{sec:related}\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n67\tThe options framework \\citep{sutton1999options} and its extensions \\citep{bacon2017option,harb2018waiting} provide a formal treatment of temporally extended actions.\n68\tIn model-free settings, action repeat \\citep{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} and FiGAR \\citep{sharma2017learning} offer simpler alternatives.\n69\tThese approaches modify the \\emph{acting} time scale but do not affect \\emph{planning}.\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n72\tThe Dreamer family \\citep{hafner2020dream,hafner2021mastering,hafner2023dreamerv3} learns a latent dynamics model (RSSM) and trains actor-critic networks entirely in imagination.\n73\tOther world-model approaches include IRIS \\citep{micheli2023transformers} and TWM \\citep{robine2023transformer}.\n74\tVariable-rate planning in world models has been explored with generally positive framing \\citep{hansen2022temporal}, but systematic failure analysis is lacking.\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in model-based RL.}\n77\tModel exploitation---where the policy exploits inaccuracies in the learned model---is a well-documented failure mode \\citep{janner2019trust,chua2018deep}.\n78\tThe ``delusional policy'' concept \\citep{lu2018non} captures scenarios where the value function becomes decoupled from achievable returns.\n79\tOur work identifies a specific \\emph{new} pathway to this failure through temporal abstraction's dt-embedding mechanism, distinct from the general model exploitation literature.\n80\t\n81\t%============================================================\n82\t\\section{Methodology}\n83\t\\label{sec:method}\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Base Architecture: DreamerV3}\n86\t\n87\tWe use DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} with default hyperparameters as the base system.\n88\tThe RSSM world model learns deterministic and stochastic state transitions from environment interactions; the actor and critic are trained entirely in imagination using $\\lambda$-returns computed from imagined rollouts.\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination}\n91\t\n92\tWe introduce a dt-embedding $e_{\\Delta t} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ that conditions the RSSM transition function on the elapsed time step:\n93\t\\begin{equation}\n94\t    h_t = f_\\theta(h_{t-1}, z_{t-1}, a_{t-1}, e_{\\Delta t})\n95\t\\end{equation}\n96\twhere $h_t$ is the deterministic state, $z_{t-1}$ the stochastic state, and $a_{t-1}$ the action.\n97\tDuring imagination, the actor-critic receives both the latent state and $e_{\\Delta t}$, enabling the value function to condition on the temporal resolution.\n98\t\n99\t\\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}\n100\tWhen subsampling is ON, training sequences include transitions at both $\\text{dt}=1$ and $\\text{dt}=2$ by skipping intermediate frames with probability 0.5.\n101\tWhen OFF, only consecutive ($\\text{dt}=1$) transitions are used.\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Factorial Experiment Design}\n104\t\n105\tWe construct a controlled ablation grid varying three factors:\n106\t\n107\t\\begin{center}\n108\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n109\t\\toprule\n110\t\\textbf{Factor} & \\textbf{Level 1} & \\textbf{Level 2} \\\\\n111\t\\midrule\n112\tdt-embedding & Trained & Untrained (frozen random) / Removed \\\\\n113\tSubsampling & ON (dt$\\in\\{1,2\\}$) & OFF (dt$=$1 only) \\\\\n114\tImagination horizon & H$=$8 & H$=$15 \\\\\n115\t\\bottomrule\n116\t\\end{tabular}\n117\t\\end{center}\n118\t\n119\tKey configurations (Table~\\ref{tab:configs}) are chosen to isolate each factor while holding others constant.\n120\tThe critical causal comparison is \\textbf{008d} (trained dt-embedding, subsample ON, H$=$8) vs.\\ \\textbf{008f} (dt-embedding removed, subsample ON, H$=$8), which isolates the dt-embedding while holding training data composition fixed.\n121\t\n122\t\\begin{table}[t]\n123\t\\centering\n124\t\\caption{Key experimental configurations. All use DreamerV3 base with dt$=$2 imagination unless noted.}\n125\t\\label{tab:configs}\n126\t\\small\n127\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n128\t\\toprule\n129\t\\textbf{ID} & \\textbf{Description} & \\textbf{dt-emb} & \\textbf{Subsample} & \\textbf{H} \\\\\n130\t\\midrule\n131\tBaseline & Standard DreamerV3 (dt$=$1) & --- & OFF & 15 \\\\\n132\t008d & Delusional critic config & Trained & ON & 8 \\\\\n133\t008f & Causal control (no dt-emb) & Removed & ON & 8 \\\\\n134\t008a & Value suppression config & Untrained & OFF & 15 \\\\\n135\t008e\\_v3 & Mitigation (dt$=$1 imagination) & Trained & ON & 15 \\\\\n136\t006 & Fixed dt$=$2 reference & Trained & ON & 15 \\\\\n137\t\\bottomrule\n138\t\\end{tabular}\n139\t\\end{table}\n140\t\n141\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n142\t\n143\t\\paragraph{Normalized value/eval ratio (nve).}\n144\tWe define the normalized v/e ratio as the experiment's value-to-eval ratio divided by the baseline's ratio at the same training step:\n145\t\\begin{equation}\n146\t    \\text{nve}(k) = \\frac{V_{\\text{exp}}(k) / R_{\\text{exp}}(k)}{V_{\\text{base}}(k) / R_{\\text{base}}(k)}\n147\t\\end{equation}\n148\tValues above 1.0 indicate overestimation relative to baseline; we identify $\\text{nve} > 1.5\\times$ at steps $\\geq$35K as the \\textbf{pathological threshold}.\n149\tThe 35K lower bound ensures baseline v/e has converged sufficiently (baseline v/e $>0.24$ at 35K) to yield stable ratios.\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ThN6WpRhxnFZ4u1h4UFPY2","session_id":"31787645-5970-4613-84a7-699e9017e33d","uuid":"61e277a2-d7ca-4d17-a6fb-928c20b5a59a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T01:51:10.775Z"}
