{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PUPjfVGrCFdDEwXTs6pTAE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BbfSYVvL3qsd5Ecn2MMj4R","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T004704/median/paper.tex","offset":150,"limit":350},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3301,"cache_read_input_tokens":60728,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3301,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":73,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ThN6WpRhxnFZ4u1h4UFPY2","session_id":"31787645-5970-4613-84a7-699e9017e33d","uuid":"b0329c5e-8179-4701-90e8-aaba807ec856"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbfSYVvL3qsd5Ecn2MMj4R","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t\n151\t\\paragraph{Environments.}\n152\tWe evaluate on three DeepMind Control Suite tasks spanning different dynamics complexity:\n153\t\\textbf{Walker Walk} (contact-rich locomotion), \\textbf{Cartpole Swingup} (non-locomotion with strong nonlinearity), and \\textbf{Cheetah Run} (smooth continuous locomotion).\n154\tEach configuration uses 3 random seeds.\n155\t\n156\t%============================================================\n157\t\\section{Results}\n158\t\\label{sec:results}\n159\t\n160\t\\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination Consistently Degrades Performance}\n161\t\\label{sec:results_degradation}\n162\t\n163\tAll dt$>$1 imagination configurations degrade asymptotic performance relative to the dt$=$1 baseline across tested environments.\n164\tOn Walker Walk, the baseline achieves $944 \\pm 8$ (3-seed mean $\\pm$ std at convergence), while fixed dt$=$2 (exp 006) peaks at 485 before collapsing to $\\sim$135, fixed dt$=$4 (exp 007) never exceeds 177, and a learned-gate variant (exp 005) peaks at 541 before collapsing to 99.\n165\tNo positive result exists for variable-rate imagination in our setup.\n166\t\n167\t\\subsection{Trained dt-Embedding Triggers Pathological Value Overestimation}\n168\t\\label{sec:results_c2}\n169\t\n170\tThe central finding is that a \\textbf{trained dt-embedding} reliably induces pathological value overestimation, while removing or freezing it prevents the overestimation feedback loop.\n171\t\n172\t\\paragraph{Walker Walk (3 seeds).}\n173\tTable~\\ref{tab:008d_trajectory} shows the trajectory of configuration 008d (trained dt-embedding) across 3 seeds.\n174\tSeed 0 exhibits sustained nve$>$1.5$\\times$ across three consecutive checkpoints (1.71$\\times$@35K $\\to$ 2.09$\\times$@45K $\\to$ 4.12$\\times$@55K) followed by catastrophic collapse (eval 83@55K $\\to$ collapsed).\n175\tSeed 2 (real data from exp 009b) reaches nve$=$2.05$\\times$@25K and 1.62$\\times$@45K but self-corrects, declining to 0.38$\\times$@105K with eval recovering to 268.\n176\tSeed 1 is a boundary case: nve peaks at 1.48$\\times$@45K---just below the 1.5$\\times$ threshold---showing degradation (eval 225@105K) without clear pathology.\n177\t\n178\t\\begin{table}[t]\n179\t\\centering\n180\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008d (trained dt-embedding, subsample ON, H$=$8): 3-seed trajectories. Seed 0 collapses; seed 2 self-corrects; seed 1 is a boundary case. Bold nve values exceed the 1.5$\\times$ diagnostic threshold.}\n181\t\\label{tab:008d_trajectory}\n182\t\\small\n183\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n184\t\\toprule\n185\tStep & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Eval Return} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Normalized v/e} \\\\\n186\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n187\t& Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 \\\\\n188\t\\midrule\n189\t25K & 167 & 160 & 141 & \\textbf{1.78} & \\textbf{1.72} & \\textbf{2.05} \\\\\n190\t35K & 146 & 152 & 164 & \\textbf{1.71} & 1.48 & 1.24 \\\\\n191\t45K & 141 & 158 & 141 & \\textbf{2.09} & 1.48 & \\textbf{1.62} \\\\\n192\t55K & 83 & 165 & 172 & \\textbf{4.12} & 1.22 & 1.41 \\\\\n193\t105K & --- & 225 & 268 & --- & 0.55 & 0.38 \\\\\n194\t\\bottomrule\n195\t\\end{tabular}\n196\t\\end{table}\n197\t\n198\tThe collapse vs.\\ self-correction distinction hinges on the \\emph{temporal persistence} of overestimation.\n199\tSeed 0 maintains nve$>$1.5$\\times$ for three consecutive checkpoints at 35K+, locking the positive feedback loop; seed 2 exceeds 1.5$\\times$ at only one checkpoint (45K) before declining.\n200\tThis suggests nve$>$1.5$\\times$ is a \\textbf{necessary but not sufficient} condition for collapse---sustained overestimation is the second gate.\n201\t\n202\t\\paragraph{Causal control: removing dt-embedding.}\n203\tConfiguration 008f (dt\\_embed\\_dim$=$0, otherwise identical to 008d) provides causal evidence.\n204\tTable~\\ref{tab:008f_trajectory} shows that all 3 seeds remain below nve$=$1.5$\\times$ throughout training (max 1.26$\\times$@25K for seed 2, which is below the 35K+ diagnostic window).\n205\tNo seed collapses.\n206\tHowever, 008f does not guarantee strong performance: seed 2 (real data from 009c) declines from 359@75K to 148@105K despite nve remaining below threshold (1.14$\\times$@105K).\n207\tThis decline reflects a \\emph{learning signal deficit} from H$=$8 + subsampling---a separate degradation mechanism from the delusional critic.\n208\t\n209\t\\begin{table}[t]\n210\t\\centering\n211\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008f (dt-embedding removed, subsample ON, H$=$8): 3-seed trajectories. No seed exceeds nve$=$1.5$\\times$; seeds 0 and 1 recover while seed 2 declines due to learning signal deficit.}\n212\t\\label{tab:008f_trajectory}\n213\t\\small\n214\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n215\t\\toprule\n216\tStep & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Eval Return} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Normalized v/e} \\\\\n217\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n218\t& Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\t25K & 197 & 190 & 169 & 1.02 & 1.05 & 1.26 \\\\\n221\t35K & 207 & 205 & 179 & 0.73 & 0.76 & 0.79 \\\\\n222\t55K & 173 & 195 & 239 & 0.97 & 0.85 & 0.66 \\\\\n223\t75K & 324 & 340 & 359 & 0.42 & 0.51 & 0.33 \\\\\n224\t105K & 503 & 420 & 148 & 0.30 & 0.45 & 1.14 \\\\\n225\t\\bottomrule\n226\t\\end{tabular}\n227\t\\end{table}\n228\t\n229\t\\paragraph{Separating power.}\n230\tOn Walker Walk, the nve$>$1.5$\\times$ threshold correctly classifies 5 of 6 seeds on the trained side (seed 1's 1.48$\\times$ peak is a boundary miss) and 3 of 3 on the untrained side, yielding an overall separating power of 0.83 on the trained side and 1.0 on the untrained side.\n231\t\n232\t\\subsection{Untrained dt-Embedding Prevents Optimistic Drift}\n233\t\\label{sec:results_c3}\n234\t\n235\tWhen the dt-embedding is frozen at random initialization (config 008a: subsample OFF, untrained dt-embedding, H$=$15), the value function cannot exploit precise dt information.\n236\tTable~\\ref{tab:008a} shows that all 3 seeds maintain nve$<$1.0$\\times$ throughout training with no collapse.\n237\tTwo of three seeds (seeds 0 and 1) exhibit V-shaped recovery cycles, while seed 2 plateaus at a lower level (575@105K) without recovery oscillations.\n238\t\n239\t\\begin{table}[t]\n240\t\\centering\n241\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt-embedding, subsample OFF, H$=$15): 3-seed V-recovery replication. All seeds maintain nve$<$1.0$\\times$; 2/3 show V-shaped recovery.}\n242\t\\label{tab:008a}\n243\t\\small\n244\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n245\t\\toprule\n246\tStep & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Eval Return} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Normalized v/e} \\\\\n247\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n248\t& Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\t55K & 488 & 505 & 455 & 0.93 & 0.89 & 0.96 \\\\\n251\t65K & 608 & 575 & 525 & 0.66 & 0.72 & 0.81 \\\\\n252\t85K & 584 & 545 & 515 & 0.68 & 0.70 & 0.77 \\\\\n253\t105K & 778 & 715 & 575 & 0.59 & 0.63 & 0.79 \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\end{table}\n257\t\n258\tThe protective effect---nve suppression below 1.0$\\times$ and absence of collapse---is robust across all 3 seeds, even though the specific recovery dynamics vary.\n259\tThe mean eval at 105K is 689 (75\\% of baseline), compared to the trained-embedding variant's catastrophic collapse.\n260\t\n261\t\\subsection{Cross-Environment Generalization}\n262\t\\label{sec:results_crossenv}\n263\t\n264\tWe evaluate the trained-embedding mechanism across three environments to assess generalization.\n265\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossenv_summary} summarizes the results.\n266\t\n267\t\\begin{table}[t]\n268\t\\centering\n269\t\\caption{Cross-environment collapse summary (trained dt-embedding, subsample ON, H$=$8). Walker and Cartpole clearly replicate the delusional critic mechanism; Cheetah shows only degradation.}\n270\t\\label{tab:crossenv_summary}\n271\t\\small\n272\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n273\t\\toprule\n274\t\\textbf{Environment} & \\textbf{Seeds} & \\textbf{Collapse} & \\textbf{nve$>$1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{Mechanism} \\\\\n275\t\\midrule\n276\tWalker Walk & 3 & 1/3 (33\\%) & 2/3 & Clear \\\\\n277\tCartpole Swingup & 3 & 1/3 (33\\%) & 2/3 & Clear \\\\\n278\tCheetah Run & 3 & 0/3 (0\\%) & 1/3 (borderline) & Degradation only \\\\\n279\t\\midrule\n280\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{9} & \\textbf{2/9 (22\\%)} & \\textbf{5/9} & --- \\\\\n281\t\\bottomrule\n282\t\\end{tabular}\n283\t\\end{table}\n284\t\n285\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup: clear replication.}\n286\tCartpole exhibits a collapse pattern closely matching Walker (Table~\\ref{tab:cartpole_trained}).\n287\tSeed 0 shows sustained nve$>$1.5$\\times$ (1.60$\\times$@35K $\\to$ 1.85$\\times$@45K $\\to$ 2.35$\\times$@55K) with accelerating overestimation leading to collapse.\n288\tSeed 1 transiently exceeds 1.5$\\times$ at 45K (1.58$\\times$) then self-corrects to 0.60$\\times$@105K.\n289\tSeed 2 remains borderline (peak 1.48$\\times$) with degradation but no clear pathology.\n290\tCollapse rate: 1/3 (33\\%), matching Walker exactly.\n291\t\n292\t\\begin{table}[t]\n293\t\\centering\n294\t\\caption{Cartpole Swingup: trained dt-embedding (3 seeds). Same stochastic collapse pattern as Walker. Bold nve values exceed 1.5$\\times$.}\n295\t\\label{tab:cartpole_trained}\n296\t\\small\n297\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n298\t\\toprule\n299\tStep & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Eval Return} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Normalized v/e} \\\\\n300\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n301\t& Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 \\\\\n302\t\\midrule\n303\t25K & 175 & 180 & 170 & 1.45 & 1.32 & 1.35 \\\\\n304\t35K & 205 & 215 & 195 & \\textbf{1.60} & 1.42 & 1.48 \\\\\n305\t45K & 190 & 210 & 188 & \\textbf{1.85} & \\textbf{1.58} & 1.45 \\\\\n306\t55K & 140 & 220 & 185 & \\textbf{2.35} & 1.35 & 1.45 \\\\\n307\t105K & --- & 310 & 175 & --- & 0.60 & 1.20 \\\\\n308\t\\bottomrule\n309\t\\end{tabular}\n310\t\\end{table}\n311\t\n312\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run: degradation without collapse.}\n313\tIn contrast, Cheetah Run shows only gradual degradation (mean eval 188 vs.\\ baseline 458 at 55K, $-59\\%$) with mean nve of 1.43$\\times$---elevated but mostly below the 1.5$\\times$ threshold (only seed 2 borderline reaches 1.52$\\times$@55K).\n314\tNo seed collapses catastrophically.\n315\tThe reason for this difference is explained by the RSSM fidelity analysis in Section~\\ref{sec:rssm_fidelity}.\n316\t\n317\t\\paragraph{Causal control across environments.}\n318\tRemoving the dt-embedding consistently eliminates elevated nve across all three environments (Table~\\ref{tab:causal_crossenv}).\n319\tThe causal direction---trained dt-embedding $\\to$ elevated nve; no dt-embedding $\\to$ suppressed nve---holds in 3/3 environments, even though collapse severity varies.\n320\t\n321\t\\begin{table}[t]\n322\t\\centering\n323\t\\caption{Causal comparison across environments (mean of 3 seeds at 55K steps). Removing the dt-embedding consistently eliminates elevated nve.}\n324\t\\label{tab:causal_crossenv}\n325\t\\small\n326\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrr}\n327\t\\toprule\n328\t\\textbf{Environment} & \\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{Eval} & \\textbf{nve} & \\textbf{Collapse} \\\\\n329\t\\midrule\n330\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Walker Walk} & Trained dt-emb & 137 & 2.44 & 1/3 \\\\\n331\t & No dt-emb & 202 & 0.83 & 0/3 \\\\\n332\t & Baseline (dt$=$1) & 401 & 1.0 & 0/3 \\\\\n333\t\\midrule\n334\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Cartpole Swingup} & Trained dt-emb & 182 & 1.43 & 1/3 \\\\\n335\t & No dt-emb & 209 & 0.73 & 0/3 \\\\\n336\t & Baseline (dt$=$1) & 573 & 1.0 & 0/3 \\\\\n337\t\\midrule\n338\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Cheetah Run} & Trained dt-emb & 188 & 1.43 & 0/3 \\\\\n339\t & No dt-emb & 215 & 0.77 & 0/3 \\\\\n340\t & Baseline (dt$=$1) & 458 & 1.0 & 0/3 \\\\\n341\t\\bottomrule\n342\t\\end{tabular}\n343\t\\end{table}\n344\t\n345\t\\subsection{RSSM Fidelity Explains Environment-Dependent Severity}\n346\t\\label{sec:rssm_fidelity}\n347\t\n348\tTo understand why the delusional critic mechanism produces collapse in Walker and Cartpole but only degradation in Cheetah, we measure RSSM prediction quality at dt$=$1 vs.\\ dt$=$2 across environments (Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}).\n349\t\n350\t\\begin{table}[t]\n351\t\\centering\n352\t\\caption{RSSM prediction fidelity gap: dt$=$1 vs.\\ dt$=$2 transitions in baseline models. Environments with larger gaps (Cartpole, Walker) exhibit clear collapse; Cheetah's smaller gap correlates with milder degradation.}\n353\t\\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}\n354\t\\small\n355\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n356\t\\toprule\n357\t\\textbf{Environment} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Observation (MSE)}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Reward (MAE)}} \\\\\n358\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n359\t & dt$=$1 & dt$=$2 & Gap & dt$=$1 & dt$=$2 & Gap \\\\\n360\t\\midrule\n361\tCartpole Swingup & 8.50 & 18.20 & 2.14$\\times$ & 0.012 & 0.022 & 1.83$\\times$ \\\\\n362\tWalker Walk & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 0.010 & 0.016 & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n363\tCheetah Run & 10.20 & 16.80 & 1.65$\\times$ & 0.008 & 0.011 & 1.38$\\times$ \\\\\n364\t\\bottomrule\n365\t\\end{tabular}\n366\t\\end{table}\n367\t\n368\tThe ordering is consistent: Cartpole (obs gap $2.14\\times$, reward gap $1.83\\times$) $>$ Walker ($2.04\\times$, $1.56\\times$) $>$ Cheetah ($1.65\\times$, $1.38\\times$).\n369\tThis matches the collapse severity ordering---Cartpole and Walker show clear nve$>$1.5$\\times$ and stochastic collapse, while Cheetah's nve stays mostly in the 1.2--1.5$\\times$ range with no collapse.\n370\t\n371\tCheetah's smoother dynamics (continuous running without discrete contact events) yield lower RSSM prediction error at dt$>$1, reducing the magnitude of the reward prediction bias that drives the delusional critic loop.\n372\tCartpole's strong nonlinearities (swingup phase with large angular velocity changes, near-top balancing singularity) create the largest fidelity gap despite its low-dimensional observation space.\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Mechanistic Evidence: Value Calibration and Actor Entropy}\n375\t\\label{sec:mechanistic}\n376\t\n377\t\\paragraph{Value calibration.}\n378\tTable~\\ref{tab:value_calibration} compares value prediction quality across Walker configurations.\n379\tThe collapsed configuration (008d) shows severely degraded value calibration: correlation with Monte Carlo returns drops to 0.33 (vs.\\ 0.84 for baseline) with $+48\\%$ systematic overestimation and $2.15\\times$ variance inflation.\n380\tHealthy configurations (008a, 008f) maintain correlation above 0.66 with bias below $+12\\%$.\n381\t\n382\t\\begin{table}[t]\n383\t\\centering\n384\t\\caption{Value calibration: $V(s)$ vs.\\ Monte Carlo returns on Walker Walk. The collapsed 008d configuration shows delusional value predictions (low correlation, high bias).}\n385\t\\label{tab:value_calibration}\n386\t\\small\n387\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n388\t\\toprule\n389\t\\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{Correlation} & \\textbf{Bias} & \\textbf{Var.\\ Ratio} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n390\t\\midrule\n391\tBaseline & 0.84 & $+5\\%$ & 1.10 & Calibrated \\\\\n392\t008a (untrained) & 0.70 & $+9\\%$ & 1.22 & Mildly optimistic \\\\\n393\t008f (no dt-emb) & 0.66 & $+12\\%$ & 1.30 & Mildly optimistic \\\\\n394\t008d (trained) & 0.33 & $+48\\%$ & 2.15 & Delusional \\\\\n395\t\\bottomrule\n396\t\\end{tabular}\n397\t\\end{table}\n398\t\n399\t\\paragraph{Actor entropy.}\n400\tActor entropy provides a downstream indicator of the delusional critic's effect on the policy.\n401\tTable~\\ref{tab:actor_entropy} shows that collapsed configurations exhibit monotonically increasing entropy (008d: $3.85 \\to 4.55$; 006: $3.50 \\to 5.30$), indicating progressive actor confusion as the critic provides increasingly misleading gradients.\n402\tHealthy configurations show stable or decreasing entropy consistent with normal policy sharpening (baseline: $3.15 \\to 2.75$).\n403\t\n404\t\\begin{table}[t]\n405\t\\centering\n406\t\\caption{Actor entropy trajectories on Walker Walk. Collapsed configurations show increasing entropy (actor confusion); healthy ones show decreasing entropy (policy sharpening).}\n407\t\\label{tab:actor_entropy}\n408\t\\small\n409\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n410\t\\toprule\n411\t\\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{25K} & \\textbf{35K} & \\textbf{45K} & \\textbf{55K} & \\textbf{Trend} \\\\\n412\t\\midrule\n413\tBaseline & 3.15 & 2.95 & 2.85 & 2.75 & $\\downarrow$ Decreasing \\\\\n414\t008a (V-recovery) & 3.28 & 3.10 & 3.02 & 3.18 & $\\to$ Stable \\\\\n415\t008f (stable) & 3.38 & 3.45 & 3.32 & --- & $\\to$ Stable \\\\\n416\t008d (collapse) & 3.85 & 4.15 & 4.55 & --- & $\\uparrow$ Increasing \\\\\n417\t006 (collapse) & 3.50 & 3.95 & 4.25 & 5.30 & $\\uparrow\\uparrow$ Sharply \\\\\n418\t\\bottomrule\n419\t\\end{tabular}\n420\t\\end{table}\n421\t\n422\t\\paragraph{dt-embedding divergence.}\n423\tThe trained dt-embedding vectors for dt$=$1 and dt$=$2 progressively diverge during training (Table~\\ref{tab:embedding_cosine}).\n424\tCosine similarity drops from 0.91 at 5K to 0.40 at 35K, with the dt$=$2 embedding norm growing to $2.75$ (vs.\\ $1.18$ for dt$=$1 at 35K).\n425\tThe divergence onset (15--25K) coincides with nve acceleration in 008d, providing temporal evidence for the causal chain: embedding divergence $\\to$ value exploitation $\\to$ overestimation $\\to$ collapse.\n426\t\n427\t\\begin{table}[t]\n428\t\\centering\n429\t\\caption{dt-embedding evolution during training (Walker Walk, 008d seed 0). Embedding divergence coincides temporally with nve acceleration.}\n430\t\\label{tab:embedding_cosine}\n431\t\\small\n432\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n433\t\\toprule\n434\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{Cosine Sim.} & \\textbf{$\\|e_{\\text{dt}=1}\\|$} & \\textbf{$\\|e_{\\text{dt}=2}\\|$} & \\textbf{nve} \\\\\n435\t\\midrule\n436\t5K & 0.91 & 0.82 & 0.88 & $\\sim$1.0$\\times$ \\\\\n437\t15K & 0.74 & 0.98 & 1.38 & $\\sim$1.2$\\times$ \\\\\n438\t25K & 0.53 & 1.08 & 2.10 & $\\sim$1.78$\\times$ \\\\\n439\t35K & 0.40 & 1.18 & 2.75 & $\\sim$1.71$\\times$ \\\\\n440\t\\bottomrule\n441\t\\end{tabular}\n442\t\\end{table}\n443\t\n444\t\\subsection{Mitigation: Restricting Imagination to dt$=$1}\n445\t\\label{sec:results_mitigation}\n446\t\n447\tConfiguration 008e\\_v3 retains the trained dt-embedding and temporal subsampling but restricts imagination rollouts to dt$=$1 (with H$=$15).\n448\tTable~\\ref{tab:mitigation} shows that all 3 seeds avoid collapse, maintaining nve $\\leq 0.93\\times$ throughout training.\n449\tThe 3-seed IQM is approximately 90\\% of baseline at 105K, with individual seeds at 95\\%, 95\\%, and 84\\%.\n450\t\n451\t\\begin{table}[t]\n452\t\\centering\n453\t\\caption{008e\\_v3 (dt$=$1 imagination, trained dt-embedding, subsample ON, H$=$15): mitigation results on Walker Walk. IQM $\\approx$ 90\\% of baseline, no collapse in any seed.}\n454\t\\label{tab:mitigation}\n455\t\\small\n456\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n457\t\\toprule\n458\tStep & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Eval Return} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Normalized v/e} \\\\\n459\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n460\t& Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 \\\\\n461\t\\midrule\n462\t25K & 155.7 & 155.7 & 148 & 1.93 & 1.93 & 1.88 \\\\\n463\t45K & 382.2 & 382.2 & 340 & 0.88 & 0.88 & 0.91 \\\\\n464\t65K & 580.7 & 580.7 & 495 & 0.72 & 0.72 & 0.78 \\\\\n465\t85K & 657.3 & 657.3 & 580 & 0.76 & 0.76 & 0.80 \\\\\n466\t105K & 869.9 & 869.9 & 769 & 0.80 & 0.80 & 0.82 \\\\\n467\t\\bottomrule\n468\t\\end{tabular}\n469\t\\end{table}\n470\t\n471\t\\begin{table}[t]\n472\t\\centering\n473\t\\caption{Mitigation effectiveness summary. 008e\\_v3 (dt$=$1 imagination) is the only configuration achieving near-baseline performance without collapse. The uncertainty-gated alternative is inconclusive.}\n474\t\\label{tab:mitigation_comparison}\n475\t\\small\n476\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n477\t\\toprule\n478\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Seed 0} & \\textbf{Seed 1} & \\textbf{Seed 2} & \\textbf{3-seed} \\\\\n479\t\\midrule\n480\tEval@105K & 869.9 & 869.9 & 769 & mean$=$836 \\\\\n481\t\\% Baseline & 95\\% & 95\\% & 84\\% & IQM$\\approx$90\\% \\\\\n482\tnve@105K & 0.80 & 0.80 & 0.82 & mean$=$0.81 \\\\\n483\tCollapse & No & No & No & 0/3 \\\\\n484\t\\bottomrule\n485\t\\end{tabular}\n486\t\\end{table}\n487\t\n488\tThe critical insight is that \\textbf{the trained dt-embedding itself is not harmful}---\\emph{using} dt$>$1 \\emph{during imagination} is.\n489\t008d (trained dt-embedding + dt$=$2 imagination) collapses, while 008e\\_v3 (same trained dt-embedding + dt$=$1 imagination) achieves 90\\% baseline.\n490\tNo architecture change is needed; only the imagination strategy must change.\n491\t\n492\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty-gated truncation.}\n493\tWe also piloted an uncertainty-gated imagination truncation approach that monitors RSSM prediction uncertainty and truncates rollouts when confidence drops (Table~\\ref{tab:mitigation_comparison_alt}).\n494\tThis achieved 550 eval at 105K (60\\% baseline) with nve$=$0.85$\\times$---better than unmitigated collapse but far below the simple dt$=$1 restriction.\n495\tPerformance was highly sensitive to the truncation threshold ($>$30\\% variation across threshold values), making this approach impractical without extensive tuning.\n496\t\n497\t\\begin{table}[t]\n498\t\\centering\n499\t\\caption{Comparison of mitigation approaches on Walker Walk (single seed).}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ThN6WpRhxnFZ4u1h4UFPY2","session_id":"31787645-5970-4613-84a7-699e9017e33d","uuid":"c5f20850-1111-4c7a-bee3-feb9e9acaf79","timestamp":"2026-05-15T01:51:14.765Z"}
