{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ab73c4ad517e43ec4","tool_use_id":"toolu_01L4jssAZWLAQJYMSAJSFtZm","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":73147,"tool_uses":13,"duration_ms":107525},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"f7ddd432-5106-45e1-8124-c94a70f147ce","session_id":"31787645-5970-4613-84a7-699e9017e33d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01ByoyEWMjW4jp8999kxW1ze","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BEdncjuzKTTkYUzUteqG4L","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"event-gated-adaptive-horizon","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260515T004704","overall_score":"3 - Weak Reject","report":"# Reviewer R1: Methods and Experiments\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper diagnoses why variable-rate imagination fails in DreamerV3, identifying a causal chain from trained duration embeddings through RSSM fidelity gaps to systematic value overestimation (\"delusional critic\"). The nve diagnostic marker achieves 18/18 classification accuracy across three DMC environments. A C6 mitigation (dt=1 imagination) recovers 94.8% baseline in Walker Walk.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Well-structured factorial design.** The three-factor manipulation (dt_emb status, subsampling, imagination dt) with appropriate controls systematically isolates the causal variable. The progression from 008d vs 008f (trained vs removed dt_emb) to 008e_v3 (trained dt_emb + dt=1 imagination) is a clean causal decomposition.\n\n- **Strong diagnostic signal.** The nve>1.5x threshold achieves perfect separation (18/18) across three environments between trained and untrained/removed dt_emb configurations. The gap between the two groups is large (trained peaks 1.52--2.35x vs untrained peaks 0.98--1.26x), providing a wide margin around the threshold.\n\n- **Honest treatment of stochastic collapse.** The paper correctly distinguishes between the reliable diagnostic marker (nve>1.5x, 9/9 trained seeds) and the stochastic consequence (collapse, 4/9 seeds). This is more defensible than claiming deterministic collapse.\n\n- **Multi-level mechanistic evidence.** The causal chain is supported at multiple levels: RSSM fidelity gap (Table 2), embedding divergence dynamics (Table 3), value calibration trajectories (Tables 4--7), KL divergence of value distributions (Table 11), and actor entropy analysis. These are largely independent diagnostic signals that converge on the same conclusion.\n\n- **Practical mitigation with tight CI.** C6 IQM=870 [852, 888] in Walker is a convincing result. The 94.8% recovery with zero implementation cost makes this immediately actionable.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **Critical: Most experiments are imagined, not executed.** The decisions.yaml and memory.md reveal that only Walker Walk experiments (and partially) have been run. The Cheetah, Cartpole, and Hopper data appear to be \"best-tier\" projections from a dream/simulation framework, not actual experimental results. The imagined_experiments.yaml file explicitly labels these as \"expected_results\" with \"result_rationale\" explaining why they *should* turn out a certain way. This is a fatal concern if the paper presents imagined data as real.\n\n- **3 seeds is marginal for the claims made.** The 100% classification claim (18/18) sounds impressive but with only 9 trained and 9 untrained data points across 3 environments, a single misclassification drops accuracy to 94%. The Hopper data already shows this fragility (5/6 = 83%). A Fisher exact test on the 2x2 table (9/9 vs 0/9 exceeding threshold) gives p < 0.001, which is reassuring, but the point estimate of 100% is unstable with this sample size.\n\n- **The 1.5x threshold appears post-hoc.** The paper does not justify why 1.5x rather than 1.3x or 1.8x. The decisions trail (D032) reveals this threshold was discovered empirically by observing the data and finding \"perfect separation.\" This is textbook overfitting to the training set. Without a held-out validation set or a principled derivation, the 100% accuracy claim is inflated.\n\n- **C6 mitigation validated only in Walker Walk.** The crown jewel mitigation (dt=1 imagination) has 3-seed validation in only one environment. If the mechanism is truly architecture-level, C6 should replicate in Cheetah and Cartpole. Its absence is conspicuous.\n\n- **No figures in a diagnostic paper.** A paper whose core contribution is identifying temporal dynamics of value overestimation has zero figures. The nve trajectories, collapse bifurcation, RSSM fidelity gap scatter, and embedding divergence timeline all cry out for visualization. Tables alone cannot convey the temporal dynamics that are central to the narrative. This significantly weakens both clarity and persuasiveness.\n\n- **The \"closed causal chain\" overstates the evidence.** The paper establishes strong correlation and controlled ablation (necessary conditions), but the chain \"trained dt_emb -> RSSM exploits fidelity gap -> value overestimation -> actor collapse\" has a gap: removing dt_emb also degrades reward prediction by 75x (memory.md), introducing a massive confound. The ablation does not cleanly remove only the \"exploitation\" channel; it removes the reward prediction capacity simultaneously. An intervention that disables dt_emb exploitation *without* degrading reward prediction would close the loop more convincingly.\n\n- **Confound in the \"no dt_emb\" control acknowledged but unresolved.** The decisions trail (D023, D030) explicitly acknowledges that dt_embed_dim=0 causes 75x reward MAE degradation. This means the \"no dt_emb\" condition is not a clean control -- it simultaneously removes the hypothesized causal mechanism AND degrades the learning signal. The paper should discuss this more prominently.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. How much of the Cheetah/Cartpole/Hopper data is from actual completed experiments vs. projected \"best-tier\" estimates? Please clarify which table entries are real measurements.\n\n2. What is the sensitivity of the 18/18 classification to the threshold value? At what threshold does the first misclassification occur? A ROC curve would be informative.\n\n3. The C6 mitigation retains multi-dt training data but uses dt=1 imagination. What is the purpose of the multi-dt training data if imagination never uses dt>1? Is there evidence it helps vs. a pure dt=1 baseline?\n\n4. Why is C6 evaluated at 105K steps rather than 500K? The baseline IQM at 105K is 918 vs. 947 at 500K. Does C6 maintain the 94.8% ratio at convergence?\n\n5. Can you provide an ablation that disables dt_emb exploitation of the fidelity gap without destroying reward prediction quality -- for instance, detaching dt_emb gradients from the reward head only?\n\n## Preliminary Score and Justification\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n**Confidence: 3/5**\n\nThe diagnostic framework is intellectually compelling, and the Walker Walk results are genuinely interesting. The causal decomposition via factorial design is well-conceived. However, several issues prevent acceptance at a top venue: (1) the provenance of Cheetah/Cartpole/Hopper results is unclear and may be projections rather than measurements; (2) the 1.5x threshold is post-hoc with no held-out validation; (3) C6 is validated in only one environment; (4) the \"no dt_emb\" control has a massive confound (75x reward degradation); and (5) the complete absence of figures in a dynamics-focused diagnostic paper is a significant presentation failure. If the cross-environment data is confirmed as real and the threshold is validated on held-out environments, this could move to a 4 or 5 in revision."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":705,"cache_read_input_tokens":72142,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":705,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01L4jssAZWLAQJYMSAJSFtZm","session_id":"31787645-5970-4613-84a7-699e9017e33d","uuid":"35235959-860a-4afb-87cf-f052559fc31c"}
